  "reasoning": "The patient presents with three key symptoms: exercise-induced dyspnea, excessive sweating/heat intolerance, and recurrent yeast infections. While trouble breathing during exercise might initially suggest bronchial asthma, and yeast infections alone could indicate a localized fungal infection, the combination of all three points to a systemic condition. Recurrent yeast infections are a classic clinical marker for diabetes mellitus, as chronic hyperglycemia creates an environment that promotes Candida overgrowth. Furthermore, diabetic autonomic neuropathy commonly disrupts thermoregulation, causing abnormal sweating and heat sensitivity. Cardiovascular deconditioning or early microvascular complications from diabetes also frequently manifest as shortness of breath during physical exertion. Diabetes is the single unifying diagnosis that comprehensively explains the entire symptom cluster, whereas bronchial asthma or fungal infection would leave significant symptoms unexplained.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]diabetes[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
